exercises.task:
Java:
class Animal {
  void speak() { System.out.println("..."); }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
  @Override
  void speak() { System.out.println("woof"); }
}
Animal a = new Dog();
a.speak(); // woof

Do the same override in JavaScript (no @Override needed) and print the same line.

exercises.solution:
class Animal {
  speak() { console.log("..."); }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
  speak() { console.log("woof"); }
}
class Cat extends Animal {
  speak() { console.log("meow"); }
}
const pets = [new Dog(), new Cat()];
for (const p of pets) {
  p.speak();
}

